From: Jahshaka <jahshaka@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] HDR: a NaN measurement must not latch the adapted luminance forever

WHY (Jahshaka lane SMOKE-ENGINE-1 item 1, 2026-09-14 — measured)

HDR/DownScale03_SumLumEnd is the only RECURRENCE in the HDR chain: it writes a
1x1 keep_content texture and reads that same texture back on the next frame
(`mix( newLum, oldLum, ... )`). Every other stage is recomputed from the scene
each frame and therefore recovers by itself.

An +Inf measurement is survivable — `clamp( fLumAvg, exposure.y, exposure.z )`
pins it to exposure.z and the next frame carries on. A NaN is NOT: clamp() is
min(max(x,lo),hi), and max(NaN,lo) returns NaN on every driver we measured, so
exp(NaN) is NaN, mix(NaN, oldLum, w) is NaN, and the 1x1 history is NaN from
then on. The chain has no way back, because nothing else writes that texture
after the initial clear.

Measured in Jahshaka's editor and player on Ogre's own Hdr / ShadowMapFromCode
ports: entering the Player on a bright scene froze the adapted luminance from
frame one and it was still frozen 600+ frames later; only a WORKSPACE REBUILD
(any change that re-creates the 1x1 target and re-runs its initial clear)
brought the exposure back. The scene colour that seeded it was a half-float
overflow — a punctual light's specular lobe on a near-mirror surface goes as
1 / (pi * alpha^2), so a roughness near the shader's 0.02 floor reaches ~1e6 and
is stored in the RGBA16F scene target as +Inf, and a tile holding both +Inf and
-Inf sums to NaN.

THE FIX: two guards, both of the form `x == x`, which is false for a NaN and
true for everything else — Inf included. A chain that was already producing
numbers produces EXACTLY the same numbers, bit for bit; a chain that produced a
NaN recovers on the next frame instead of never.

  * fLumAvg falls back to exposure.y, the log-luminance floor the clamp on the
    very next line already uses: an unusable measurement reads as "a dark
    frame" for one frame.
  * oldLum falls back to newLum: a history that has already been poisoned is
    dropped in favour of this frame's measurement rather than mixed forever.

DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED HERE, because it is not this pass's business and because
changing it MOVES EVERY EXISTING PICTURE: FilmicTonemap(Inf) is Inf/Inf == NaN
in FinalToneMapping, and DownScale01_SumLumStart takes log() of whatever the
scene holds. Both were tried in this lane and measured on the Shadow Maps port:
holding the scene colour at a finite ceiling before the tonemapper removes the
black holes and replaces them with WHITE SPARKLE over 20 % of the viewport,
because that port really does produce a large population of unrepresentable
specular texels. The right fix for THOSE is upstream of the tonemapper (the
specular lobe, or the roughness floor the prepass quantises to), not a clamp in
the last pass, and it is recorded as a separate finding rather than smuggled in
here.

SCOPE: the GLSL copy only. Jahshaka runs Vulkan on every platform it ships on
(MoltenVK on macOS), so the HLSL and Metal copies of this shader are never
compiled in this tree; patching code no tree builds would be unverifiable.
Upstream would want all three languages.

MEDIA-ONLY: no C++ changes. Studio stages media from the SOURCE tree
(cmake/IncludeOgre.cmake), so every tree still needs the patch loop
(build-ogre.sh) before its staged media carries this.

diff --git a/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/DownScale03_SumLumEnd_ps.glsl b/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/DownScale03_SumLumEnd_ps.glsl
index aa5c7e82a..32c09ed47 100644
--- a/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/DownScale03_SumLumEnd_ps.glsl
+++ b/Samples/Media/2.0/scripts/materials/HDR/GLSL/DownScale03_SumLumEnd_ps.glsl
@@ -40,8 +40,27 @@ void main()
 
 	fLumAvg *= 0.25; // /= 4.0;
 
+	// JAHSHAKA: A NaN HERE IS PERMANENT, AND NOTHING ELSE IN THIS CHAIN IS.
+	//
+	// This pass writes a 1x1 keep_content texture and the mix below reads that
+	// same texture back every frame, so the adapted luminance is a recurrence
+	// with no other input. An +Inf measurement is survivable — clamp() pins it
+	// to exposure.z and the next frame recovers — but a NaN is not: clamp() is
+	// min(max(x,lo),hi), max(NaN,lo) returns NaN on every driver we measured,
+	// exp(NaN) is NaN, and mix(NaN, oldLum, w) is NaN, so ONE unusable frame is
+	// the exposure for the rest of the workspace's life. Measured in Jahshaka
+	// (2026-09-14): entering the Player on a bright scene froze the adapted
+	// luminance from frame one for 600+ frames, and only a workspace rebuild —
+	// the one thing that re-clears this texture — brought it back.
+	//
+	// `x == x` is false for a NaN and true for everything else, Inf included,
+	// so a chain that was already producing numbers produces exactly the same
+	// numbers. exposure.y is the log-luminance floor the clamp below already
+	// uses, i.e. what this pass would have written for a dark frame.
+	fLumAvg = ( fLumAvg == fLumAvg ) ? fLumAvg : exposure.y;
 	float newLum = exposure.x / exp( clamp( fLumAvg, exposure.y, exposure.z ) );
 	float oldLum = texture( vkSampler2D( oldLumRt, samplerPoint ), vec2( 0.0, 0.0 ) ).x;
+	oldLum = ( oldLum == oldLum ) ? oldLum : newLum;
 
 	//Adapt luminicense based 75% per second.
 	fragColour = mix( newLum, oldLum, pow( 0.25, timeSinceLast ) );
